1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 ( { Here absolutely the main move is } 7... Nd5 7. c4 { but in this chapter, we will have a look at } 8. h4 { and } 8. Nd2 { . While } 8. g3 { was already covered in the previous one. } ) 
8. h4 { This strange move has been popular recently, but it is more of a surprise weapon than anything else. -KEY- } Qe6 { Preparing ...d6 and ...Bxd6 or ...Bb4+ in case of c4. } 9. g3 d6 { The easiest answer. } 10. c4 Nb6 11. exd6 Bxd6 $15 { Taking with the bishop is better, because piece activity is more important than structure. The position does not require further analysis, since Black is already slightly better thanks to his better development. } 
